nalex.org/P4310320595","host_organization_name":"Wiley","host_organization_lineage":["https://openalex.org/P4310320595"],"host_organization_lineage_names":["Wiley"],"type":"journal"},"license":null,"license_id":null,"version":"publishedVersion","is_accepted":true,"is_published":true,"raw_source_name":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","raw_type":"journal-article"}],"best_oa_location":{"id":"doi:10.2307/3868449","doi":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3868449","is_oa":true,"landing_page_url":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3868449","pdf_url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1890/1540-9295%282005%29003%5B0179%3ABF%5D2.0.CO%3B2","source":{"id":"https://openalex.org/S144797041","display_name":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","issn_l":"1540-9295","issn":["1540-9295","1540-9309"],"is_oa":false,"is_in_doaj":false,"is_core":true,"listed_in":["cwts-core","dongbi-b","jufo-2","ki-jl-1","norway-2","poland-140","russia-white-list-1","vabb-shw"],"host_organization":"https://openalex.org/P4310320595","host_organization_name":"Wiley","host_organization_lineage":["https://openalex.org/P4310320595"],"host_organization_lineage_names":["Wiley"],"type":"journal"},"license":null,"license_id":null,"version":"publishedVersion","is_accepted":true,"is_published":true,"raw_source_name":"Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment","raw_type":"journal-article"},"sustainable_development_goals":[{"score":1,"id":"https://metadata.un.org/sdg/15","display_name":"Life on land"}],"sustainable_development_goals_aurora":[{"score":0.6000000238418579,"id":"https://metadata.un.org/sdg/15","display_name":"Life on land"}],"study_designs":[],"awards":[],"funders":[],"has_content":{"pdf":true,"grobid_xml":false},"content_urls":{"pdf":"https://content.openalex.org/works/W4249550477.pdf"},"referenced_works_count":0,"referenced_works":[],"related_works":["https://openalex.org/W2060514797","https://openalex.org/W1650613930","https://openalex.org/W3004670562","https://openalex.org/W2951400099","https://openalex.org/W2384345237","https://openalex.org/W2775003106","https://openalex.org/W2902127628","https://openalex.org/W2801825054","https://openalex.org/W2007201594","https://openalex.org/W3162390021"],"counts_by_year":[{"year":2025,"cited_by_count":1}],"updated_date":"2026-10-12T09:40:04.860316","created_date":"2025-10-10T00:00:00","abstract":"Diversity of life and living systems are a necessary condition for human development. As human use of resources, energy, and space intensified over the past few centuries, the diversity of life has been substantially diminished in most parts of the world. Conserving and restoring important components of the earth's biodiversity is our obligation to future generations. Biodiversity conservation is currently a principal goal for resource management of 11.5% of the world's surface area. However, substantial portions of non-human life occur outside of legally protected spaces. Even those that occur within reserves either move or have remnant populations outside. Maintaining connectivity between habitats, populations, and gene pools in and outside parks and reserves is vital for successful biodiversity conservation and restoration. Ecologists have proposed biological corridors, biosphere reserves, ecosystem management, and ecoregional planning as approaches to integrate biodiversity conservation and socioeconomic development at increasingly larger spatial scales. Ecosystem, species, and genetic level integrators appropriate to specific ecological, economic, political, social, and cultural contexts need to be identified, described, and tested in development planning and management. Globally, ecosystem services have gained attention as an integrator of biodiversity use and conservation that is amenable to economic valuation and could provide a basis for negotiations between conservation and development stakeholders. However, as the results of the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment showed, human societies tend to favor ecosystem services with a production bias, to the detriment of all others; three of the four services that have improved during the past 50 years are crop, livestock, and aquaculture production. The fourth service, enhanced carbon sequestration, has kindled hopes for linking emerging incentives in carbon markets and financing to biodiversity conservation and recovery. Critics claim that forest recovery leading to increased carbon sequestration has been confined to rich nations, and was possible only because the forests were previously exploited for production purposes. Climate change constraints and carbon-financing opportunities have emerged only in the past two or three decades. Voluntary initiatives in the financial sector to link land use, land-use change, and forestry with carbon sequestration and conservation, and recovery of biodiversity, must be seen as opportunities for using the best available ecological and other expert knowledge to improve the chances for biodiversity futures. Ecosystem services may also provide indirect ways to sustain species and genetic-level linkages between biodiversity in and around protected areas. Globally, humans are moving to the cities. The ethical and aesthetic implications of these human migrations are uncertain and open to debate. But abandoned lands in and around protected areas may be managed as wildlife habitats mixed with productive activities such as forestry, animal husbandry, and agriculture. Case studies and context-specific evidence of successful integration of biodiversity conservation at various levels and socioeconomic development at a range of spatial scales are urgently needed. Wide-ranging charismatic species almost always have spatial requirements that extend beyond parks and sanctuaries. They provide incentives and opportunities for planning and managing biodiversity at regional scales. In Sichuan, southwest China, forestry activities have been abandoned and provincial level plans to conserve and restore giant panda habitats are gaining acceptance among development planners. In 2002, following the Johannesburg Summit on Sustainable Development, UNEP and UNESCO launched the Great Apes Survival Project as a response to global concerns over the future of bonobos, chimpanzees, gorillas, and orangutans. The range of bonobos, for example, is restricted to selected parts of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, including forests lying outside legally protected areas. These initiatives will tell us whether it is possible to successfully integrate conservation and development practices. The other three great ape species, as well as many other species with large parts of their range outside protected areas, provide similar challenges and opportunities. Revival of commitments to conserve iconic species and populations, possibilities for influencing land-use change and forestry by linking ecosystem services and carbon financing, and compatibility between tourism and other productive sectors are opening up an era of options for integrating use and conservation of biodiversity. If these opportunities are to benefit both biodiversity and human society, ecologists must find new and constructive ways of working with development planners and decision makers. The Man and the Biosphere and other UNESCO programs for international cooperation are global platforms for trying out new ways of bringing conservation and development together. Natarajan Ishwaran Director, Division of Ecological and Earth Sciences, UNESCO Walter Erdelen Assistant Director-General for Natural Sciences, UNESCO","citation":"Natarajan Ishwaran & Walter R. Erdelen (2005)","page":"1"}
