ts and to the impact of the World Bank ' s GEF-funded forest sector portfolio from 1991 to June 1999. The report recommends developing policies and projects that more effectively work with sustainable forest use and management to incorporate conservation objectives, as well as expanding forest strategy goals into the private sector forest industry. The study advises that the World Bank ' s partnership with the GEF for sustainable conservation of the world ' s forest biodiversity be expanded, with more strategically developed and realistic goals and more innovative approaches FiguresreferencesRecommendeddetails View Published: October 2000ISBN: 978-0-8213-4842-0 Copyright & Permissions Related RegionsAfricaLatin America & CaribbeanRelated CountriesGuernseyRelated TopicsEnvironmentRural Development KeywordsFOREST MANAGEMENTGLOBAL ENVIRONMENTGLOBAL ENVIRONMENT FACILITYPORTFOLIO EVALUATIONPROJECT COMPONENTS DESIGNRISK ASSESSMENTTRUST FUNDSCOFINANCINGPARTICIPATORY ASSESSMENTPARTICIPATORY FORESTRYSTAKEHOLDER PARTICIPATIONFINANCING OF PROJECT COMPONENTSFINANCING PROGRAMSPOLICY IMPLEMENTATIONFORESTRY PROJECTSFOREST CONSERVATIONSOCIAL ASSESSMENTSINFORMATION MANAGEMENTENVIRONMENTAL MANAGEMENTPARTICIPATORY METHODSPROJECT DESIGNPERFORMANCE EVALUATIONFINANCIAL RESOURCESFORESTFOREST CONSERVATIONFOREST MANAGEMENTFOREST POLICYFORESTSGLOBAL BIODIVERSITYISSUESJAGUARLOGGINGMESOAMERICAN BIOLOGICAL CORRIDOR PDF downloadLoading ...","citation":"J. Gabriel Campbell & Alejandra Martin (2000)","page":"1"}
