ult in substitution of such as steel, aluminum, concrete, and plastics for wood, all of which are not only non-renewable but much more fossil fuel intensive. This article primarily presents the landscape management approach including benefits (biodiversity, reduction in CO2 emissions, increasing supply, etc.) and financing options (timber excise tax, capital gains tax, carbon sequestration swaps, etc.). 10 refs., 1 fig.","citation":"Bruce R. Lippke & Chadwick Dearing Oliver (1993)","page":"1"}
