alex.org/W2241994460","https://openalex.org/W2782232593","https://openalex.org/W1954640172","https://openalex.org/W2289336223","https://openalex.org/W2151205094","https://openalex.org/W3038536129","https://openalex.org/W2230554717","https://openalex.org/W1999559742","https://openalex.org/W2567403422"],"counts_by_year":[],"updated_date":"2025-11-06T04:12:42.849631","created_date":"2025-10-10T00:00:00","abstract":"While the Forest Sector Development Project aims at achieving sustainable management of plantations, and the conservation of biodiversity in special forest uses to contribute to natural forests, and fibre plantations, and thus reduce poverty, and enhance global environmental protection, there are main threats to biodiversity values, namely, habitat destruction, non-sustainable subsistence hunting, and gathering of non-timber forest products, and, illegal hunting and wildlife capture for commercial trade. Furthermore, soil productivity, as a function of climate action, is often disturbed by human activity, a concern regarding coastal forests and tidal mangroves, including freshwater swamp, and riparian forests. The Project design comprises four components: a) institutional development which covers a broad range of interventions to implement policies, and support regulatory and institutional framework; 2) smallholder plantation development and management, enabled by the regulatory framework set under the first component; 3) special use of forests, co-managed as set by the institutional and regulatory framework; and, 4) project management and monitoring. The project is viewed as the first phase of a longer-term development of a national, sustainable financing mechanism for biodiversity conservation in the special use of forests, and in marine and coastal ecosystems. It will build local capacity in conservation of natural resources, increase community awareness of biodiversity conservation economic opportunities, mitigate wildlife damage to agricultural crops, support alternative livelihoods in buffer zones to reduce impacts on protected areas, establish consensus-based harvesting controls, and hunting controls in buffer zones, and, build partnerships between local communities, and the local government.","citation":"John Sustainable Visions Vo Thanh Son Dick (2003)","page":"1"}
